# ls-toto — Project Overview

## What This Project Is

`ls-toto` is a faithful CLI replica of the GNU Core Utilities `ls` command.
It lists information about the FILEs given as operands (the current directory
by default), sorted alphabetically unless another sort is requested, then exits.
It does not loop and does not watch the filesystem.

Filesystem access (chosen at **build time** by the Makefile):

| Build | Mechanism | Owner / group |
|-------|-----------|---------------|
| POSIX (`dirent.h`, `sys/stat.h`, `pwd.h`, `grp.h` all available) | `opendir`/`readdir`, `lstat`/`stat`, `readlink` | `getpwuid()` / `getgrgid()`, numeric fallback |
| Windows fallback (any of those headers missing) | `FindFirstFileA`/`FindNextFileA`, `GetFileAttributesExA` | printed as `-` |

## Binary Name

The compiled binary must always be named **`ls-toto`**. Never rename it.
It is produced at **`build/ls-toto`** (MinGW may also emit
`build/ls-toto.exe`; that is the same program). The unit-test binary is
**`build/tests/test_core`**.

## Platforms

| Platform | Build environment | Notes |
|----------|-------------------|-------|
| **Linux / WSL** | `gcc`/`clang` + GNU Make | POSIX path; full unit tests |
| **Windows** | **MSYS2 UCRT64** or **Git Bash** + UCRT64 `gcc` on `PATH` | Makefile auto-selects Win32 filesystem path when the POSIX header probe fails |
| **Git Bash** | Put `/c/msys64/ucrt64/bin` **before** other toolchains on `PATH` | Avoid minimal MinGW without POSIX headers; see `toolchain-structure.mdc` |

Binaries are **not portable** across operating systems — build separately on Linux and on Windows.

## Scope — Keep It Minimal

Supporting behaviour:

- Built-in `--help` / `--h` and `--version` / `--v` (detected anywhere in `argv`; help wins if both appear)
- Core short options, combinable (`-la`), with `--` ending option parsing:

| Option | Long form | Meaning |
|--------|-----------|---------|
| `-a` | `--all` | do not ignore entries starting with `.` |
| `-A` | `--almost-all` | like `-a` but omit `.` and `..` |
| `-l` | — | long listing format |
| `-1` | — | one entry per line |
| `-r` | `--reverse` | reverse sort order |
| `-R` | `--recursive` | list subdirectories recursively |
| `-t` | — | sort by modification time, newest first |
| `-S` | — | sort by file size, largest first |
| `-h` | `--human-readable` | with `-l`, print sizes like `1.5K`, `234M` |
| `-d` | `--directory` | list directories themselves, not their contents |
| `-F` | `--classify` | append indicator (`/`, `*`, `@`, `\|`, `=`) |

- No operands → list `.`
- Operands are files or directories; file operands are listed first, then each
  directory with a `dir:` header when more than one operand is given
- Columns when stdout is a tty (width from `COLUMNS`, default 80); one name per
  line otherwise
- Name sort uses `strcmp` (C locale only)
- CLI flag strings live in `ls_toto_cli.h` (`LS_TOTO_ARG_*`); parsing is
  hand-rolled in `ls_toto_parse_args()` using `argv_has_exact()` for meta flags

**Do not add:**

- Interactive or TUI modes
- stdin reading
- `--color`, `-i`, `-n`, `-g`, `-o`, `-s`, `-U`, `-X`, `-c`, `-u`, or any other
  option outside the table above
- Locale-aware sorting or `setlocale()`
- Logging or debug output in normal operation
- Single-dash `-v` as version (`-h` is **human-readable**, never help; only
  `--help`, `--h`, `--version`, and `--v` are meta flags)
- Any feature that would break GNU `ls` behavioural compatibility for the
  supported options

## Behavioural Contract

| Invocation | Output | Exit |
|---|---|---|
| `ls-toto` | names in `.` (dotfiles hidden), sorted by name | `0` |
| `ls-toto -la` | `total N` + long lines including `.` and `..` | `0` |
| `ls-toto file dir` | `file`, blank line, `dir:` + its contents | `0` |
| `ls-toto -d dir` | `dir` itself | `0` |
| `ls-toto --version` / `--v` | prints `ls-toto <version>\n` | `0` |
| `ls-toto --help` / `--h` | prints usage on stdout | `0` |
| `ls-toto missing` | stderr: `ls-toto: cannot access 'missing': <reason>` | `2` |
| `ls-toto -z` | stderr: `ls-toto: invalid option -- 'z'` + hint | `2` |
| `ls-toto --foo` | stderr: `ls-toto: unrecognized option '--foo'` + hint | `2` |
| Subdirectory unreadable during `-R` | stderr: `ls-toto: cannot open directory '<path>': <reason>` | `1` |
| Write / allocation failure | stderr: `ls-toto: <context>: <reason>` | `2` |

`--help` anywhere in `argv` wins over `--version`. Meta flags are not
recognised after `--`.

## Exit Codes

| Code | Meaning |
|------|---------|
| `0` | Success — listing printed (or `--help` / `--version`) |
| `1` | Minor problem — e.g. cannot open a subdirectory during `-R` |
| `2` | Serious problem — inaccessible operand, invalid option, write or allocation failure |

